AI-Driven Reconfiguration of Global Human Capital Networks: A Mathematical Theory of Regional Resilience Beyond Skilled Migration

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI Collaborators: ChatGPT, Claude, DeepSeek, Meta AI

---

Abstract

Advances in artificial intelligence are substituting cognitive tasks previously reserved for skilled migrant labour. We develop a mathematical theory proving that this substitution does not merely shrink migration volumes but rewires the global human-capital network. A task-based production model yields a monotonic decline in the derived demand for foreign expertise once AI capability crosses a threshold (Theorem 1). Casting migration as a weighted directed graph, we use spectral graph theory to show that the algebraic connectivity collapses under rising automation (Theorem 2) and prove via directed percolation that the giant strongly connected component disintegrates into regional clusters at a critical automation level (Theorem 3). A mean-field game of human-capital accumulation and migration, combined with an evolutionary game of regional cooperation, demonstrates that the strategic optimum for sending countries shifts from global competition to regional integration. We characterise the transition path with entropy-regularised optimal transport and formulate a network control problem for regional resilience. The framework extends brain‑drain theory into an era of AI‑driven topological change and yields falsifiable empirical predictions.

---

1. Introduction

Globalisation has long been sustained by the movement of skilled individuals from developing to advanced economies—the so‑called brain drain. Recent evidence, however, suggests that artificial intelligence may alter this dynamic: as AI performs increasingly sophisticated cognitive tasks, the derived demand for migrant expertise in receiving countries is declining. While earlier literature has examined the substitution of labour by machines at the task level [Acemoglu & Restrepo, 2018], no unified framework captures the resulting rewiring of the global talent network at the systemic level.

This paper supplies that framework. We rigorously connect four mathematical layers: (i) a task‑based production model yielding the demand for migrant labour, (ii) a spectral‑graph description of the global migration network whose connectivity decays monotonically as automation rises, (iii) a percolation‑theoretic phase transition at which the network fragments into regional clusters, and (iv) game‑theoretic microfoundations showing that regional cooperation becomes an evolutionarily stable strategy precisely when the global market contracts. The chain of reasoning is expressed in five theorems, each grounding the next. The result is an AI Regionalisation Principle: as AI capability diffuses, the topology of human‑capital exchange shifts from a single globally connected structure to a set of internally integrated regional modules.

