2. Task‑Based Production and the Demand for Migrant Labour

We begin at the level of a receiving country’s production function.

2.1 Production Environment

Consider an advanced country j. Final output Y_j is a constant‑elasticity‑of‑substitution (CES) aggregate over a continuum of tasks x \in [0,1]:

Y_j = \left( \int_0^1 y_j(x)^{\frac{\sigma-1}{\sigma}} \, dx \right)^{\frac{\sigma}{\sigma-1}}, \qquad \sigma > 1,
\tag{1}

where \sigma is the elasticity of substitution between tasks. Each task can be produced using domestic skilled labour L_d, migrant skilled labour M, or AI capital K. The production technology for task x is linear in the effective units of the chosen input:

y_j(x) =
\begin{cases}
\gamma_L(x) \, l(x), & \text{domestic labour},\\
\gamma_M(x) \, m(x), & \text{migrants},\\
\gamma_A(x) \, A_j(t) \, k(x), & \text{AI},
\end{cases}
\tag{2}

with \gamma_L, \gamma_M, \gamma_A continuous task‑specific productivity schedules and A_j(t) the time‑varying capability of AI. Factor prices are w_L, w_M, r. Cost minimisation assigns each task to the factor offering the lowest unit cost:

\text{task } x \text{ performed by migrants} \iff \frac{w_M}{\gamma_M(x)} \le \min\left\{ \frac{w_L}{\gamma_L(x)}, \frac{r}{\gamma_A(x) A_j} \right\}.
\tag{3}

This yields a partition of [0,1] into three sets \mathcal{T}_L, \mathcal{T}_M, \mathcal{T}_{AI}, with the total quantity of migrant labour demanded being M_j = \mu(\mathcal{T}_M), where \mu is the Lebesgue measure normalised so that task output requirements are uniform.

2.2 Comparative Advantage Ordering and Wages

To track the marginal migrant task, we order tasks by the comparative advantage of migrants over AI:

Lemma 2.1 (Task ordering).
Define z(x) = \frac{\gamma_M(x)}{\gamma_A(x)}. Suppose z(x) is strictly decreasing and continuous on [0,1]. Then for any A_j>0, the set of tasks performed by migrants is a (possibly empty) interval \mathcal{T}_M = [x_1(A_j), x_2(A_j)] with 0 \le x_1 \le x_2 \le 1. Furthermore, x_1 is non‑decreasing and x_2 is non‑increasing in A_j.

Proof. From (3), migrants perform those x for which \frac{w_M}{\gamma_M(x)} \le \frac{r}{\gamma_A(x)A_j}, i.e., A_j \ge \frac{r}{w_M} \frac{\gamma_M(x)}{\gamma_A(x)} = \frac{r}{w_M} z(x). Since z(x) is decreasing, the inequality holds for the largest values of z(x), i.e. for x below a threshold. Simultaneously the condition against domestic labour defines a lower threshold, giving an interval. As A_j rises, the required z(x) threshold falls, so the interval shrinks monotonically. \square

The wage of migrant labour is determined by the marginal product at the task boundary:

w_M = \gamma_M(x_2) \cdot P, \qquad P = \left( \int_0^1 p(x)^{1-\sigma} \, dx \right)^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma}},
\tag{4}

where p(x) is the unit cost of task x and P is the CES price index. As A_j increases, x_2 shifts leftward, lowering \gamma_M(x_2) and hence w_M.

2.3 Logistic Growth of AI Capability

AI capability follows the logistic diffusion curve:

\frac{dA_j}{dt} = r A_j \left(1 - \frac{A_j}{K}\right), \quad A_j(0)=A_0<K,
\tag{5}

with r>0 and carrying capacity K. This S‑shaped trajectory ensures that A_j(t) eventually surpasses any threshold below K.

2.4 Theorem 1: AI‑Induced Decline in Demand for Migrant Labour

Theorem 1 (Monotonic decline of derived demand).
Assume (i) \sigma>1, (ii) the productivity schedules are such that \mathcal{T}_M initially has positive measure, and (iii) the comparative advantage ordering z(x) is strictly decreasing. Then there exists a critical capability A_c<K such that for all t with A_j(t)>A_c, the derived demand price D_j \equiv w_M is strictly decreasing in time:

\frac{dD_j}{dt} < 0.

Proof. From Lemma 2.1, as A_j rises continuously, the upper boundary x_2(A_j) decreases continuously. The wage w_M = \gamma_M(x_2)P inherits this monotonic decrease because \gamma_M is continuous and decreasing in x_2. The existence of A_c follows from the fact that the logistic trajectory eventually exceeds any finite value. Beyond A_c, every increase in A_j strictly contracts \mathcal{T}_M and lowers the marginal product, giving dD_j/dt < 0. \square

