3. From Task Demand to Migration Flows

Let s_{ij} be the share of country i’s skilled emigrants who would go to destination j if all destinations were equally accessible (determined by historical, linguistic, and geographic factors). The realised migration flow from i to j is proportional to the receiving country’s demand for migrant labour:

w_{ij}(t) = s_{ij} \, M_j(A_j(t)), \qquad M_j(A_j) = \mu(\mathcal{T}_M(A_j)).
\tag{6}

Equation (6) directly links the network weights to the task‑model outcomes, replacing the earlier ad‑hoc linear form. A first‑order Taylor expansion around the initial state A_j^0 yields

w_{ij}(t) \approx w_{ij}^0 \bigl(1 - \eta_j \alpha_j(t)\bigr),
\tag{7}

where \alpha_j(t) = (A_j(t)-A_j^0)/A_j^0 is the proportional increase in AI capability, \eta_j = -\frac{\partial \log M_j}{\partial \log A_j} is the elasticity of migrant demand with respect to AI, and w_{ij}^0 = s_{ij} M_j(A_j^0). This linearised form retains the essential monotonicity and suffices for the network analysis that follows.

