4. Spectral Dynamics of the Global Human‑Capital Network

4.1 Network Definition

The world is represented as a directed, weighted graph G(t)=(V,E(t),W(t)) with N countries. Edge weights are given by the matrix W(t)=[w_{ij}(t)] from (6). To study connectivity we use the undirected version obtained by symmetrisation:

\widetilde{W}(t) = \frac{1}{2}\bigl(W(t)+W(t)^\top\bigr).

The unnormalised Laplacian of this symmetrised graph is L(t) = \Delta(t) - \widetilde{W}(t), where \Delta(t) is the diagonal matrix of row sums of \widetilde{W}(t).

4.2 Algebraic Connectivity and Its Decay

The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(t) is the second smallest eigenvalue of L(t). For a connected undirected graph, \lambda_2>0; \lambda_2=0 signals disconnection. The Cheeger constant h_G satisfies the inequalities for the unnormalised Laplacian [Chung, 1997]:

\frac{h_G^2}{2\,d_{\max}} \le \lambda_2 \le 2 h_G,
\tag{8}

where d_{\max} is the maximum weighted degree.

Theorem 2 (Monotonic decline of algebraic connectivity).
Assume each \alpha_j(t) is non‑decreasing and \sup_j \alpha_j(t)\to 1 as t\to\infty. Then \lambda_2(t) is non‑increasing for all sufficiently large t, and \lim_{t\to\infty}\lambda_2(t)=0.

Proof. Because M_j decreases with A_j, all entries of \widetilde{W}(t) are non‑increasing. Adding non‑negative edge weights cannot lower \lambda_2 (Courant–Fischer). Hence decreasing them cannot increase \lambda_2. As \alpha_j\to1, edges connected to high‑automation countries vanish, so the graph approaches a set of disconnected components; therefore the Cheeger constant h_G\to0. From (8), h_G\to0 forces \lambda_2\to0. \square

