5. Percolation Phase Transition and Regional Fragmentation

The spectral decay signals a topological phase transition. We characterise it using directed percolation on the network G.

5.1 Edge Occupation Probabilities

Suppose an edge (i,j) is “active” (carries a significant flow) if the automation‑corrected weight exceeds a threshold \theta>0. Using the linearised form (7),

\text{edge }(i,j)\text{ active} \iff w_{ij}^0 (1-\eta_j\alpha_j) \ge \theta.

For sufficiently large baseline weights, this condition simplifies to 1-\eta_j\alpha_j \ge \theta', i.e. a maximum allowable automation level. We model the stochastic removal of edges by a continuous survival probability p_{ij}(\alpha_j) = f(1-\eta_j\alpha_j) where f is a smooth decreasing function. For analytical tractability, we take the linear form

p_{ij}(\alpha_j) = \max\bigl(0,\, 1 - c_{ij}\alpha_j\bigr),
\tag{9}

with constants c_{ij}>0. The average automation level is \bar{\alpha} = N^{-1}\sum_j \alpha_j.

5.2 Non‑Backtracking Matrix and GSCC Condition

The existence of a giant strongly connected component (GSCC) in a large sparse directed graph with edge probabilities p_{ij} is determined by the largest eigenvalue of the non‑backtracking matrix B [Karrer & Newman, 2011; Timár et al., 2017]. The matrix is defined on directed edges:

B_{(i\to j), (k\to l)} = \delta_{j,k}\, (1-\delta_{i,l})\, p_{k l}.
\tag{10}

A GSCC of size \Theta(N) exists if and only if \rho(B) > 1, where \rho denotes spectral radius.

Theorem 3 (Percolation threshold for fragmentation).
Under the probability model (9) and assuming the initial graph is strongly connected, there exists a critical average automation \alpha_c \in (0,1) such that the relative size S(\bar{\alpha}) of the GSCC satisfies

S(\bar{\alpha}) = 
\begin{cases}
\Theta(1), & \bar{\alpha} < \alpha_c,\\
o(1), & \bar{\alpha} > \alpha_c.
\end{cases}

Furthermore, if the baseline weights are homogeneous enough that p_{ij} depends mainly on \bar{\alpha}, a mean‑field estimate gives

\alpha_c \approx 1 - \frac{1}{\rho(B_0)},
\tag{11}

where B_0 is the non‑backtracking matrix of the graph with all p_{ij}=1.

Proof sketch. The GSCC condition \rho(B)=1 is a continuous function of the edge probabilities. As \bar{\alpha} increases, p_{ij} decrease, so \rho(B) falls. At \bar{\alpha}=0, \rho(B)=\rho(B_0)>1 by strong connectivity. At \bar{\alpha}=1, all edges fail, giving \rho(B)=0. By the intermediate value theorem, there exists \alpha_c with \rho(B(\alpha_c))=1. For networks with degree heterogeneity, the exact threshold depends on the full matrix B. In the special case where p_{ij} depends only on destination automation and the baseline graph is regular, \rho(B) \approx (1-\bar{\alpha})\rho(B_0), leading to (11). For scale‑free networks with degree exponent \gamma<3, it is known that \rho(B_0)\to\infty in the large‑N limit, implying \alpha_c\to1 (ultra‑resilience) [Cohen et al., 2001]; this yields the testable corollary that highly unequal migration networks resist fragmentation longer. \square

Post‑fragmentation, the network decomposes into strongly connected modules that largely coincide with geographic or institutional regions, forming the substrate for regional cooperation analysed in Section 7.

