6. Microfoundations: Mean‑Field Game of Human‑Capital Migration

To establish that individual optimisation indeed produces the aggregate flows (6), we formulate a mean‑field game (MFG) of human‑capital accumulation and migration.

6.1 Agent Dynamics and Controls

A continuum of agents in sending country i is indexed by human capital h\in[0,\infty). At each instant an agent chooses a control u from the set \{\text{Stay}, \text{Migrate to }j\in\mathcal{D}, \text{Migrate to regional partner }k\in\mathcal{R}\}. Human capital evolves as

dh_t = \bigl(\mu_h(u) - \delta h_t\bigr) dt + \sigma\, dW_t,
\tag{12}

with drift \mu_h depending on the working environment (S, M_j, R_k). Wages are w(u,h,\mu_t) where \mu_t is the cross‑sectional distribution. Migration to a destination j incurs a time‑dependent cost c_{ij}(t) = c_0 / (1 - \alpha_j(t)), increasing with automation as opportunities shrink.

6.2 Mean‑Field Game System

The agent’s value function V(h,t) solves the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJB) equation

-\partial_t V + \rho V = \sup_{u} \Bigl\{ w(u,h,\mu) - c_{ij}(t) \mathbf{1}_{u=M_j} + \bigl(\mu_h(u)-\delta h\bigr) \partial_h V + \frac{\sigma^2}{2} \partial_{hh} V \Bigr\},
\tag{13}

and the population density m(h,t) obeys the Fokker–Planck equation

\partial_t m + \partial_h\bigl[ (\mu_h(u^*)-\delta h) m \bigr] - \frac{\sigma^2}{2} \partial_{hh} m = 0,
\tag{14}

where u^* is the optimal control from (13). The system is closed by the consistency condition that \mu_t is the distribution of agents following u^* [Lasry & Lions, 2007; Guéant et al., 2011].

Proposition 1 (Equilibrium migration shift).
As \alpha_j(t) increases, the optimal control in the MFG equilibrium shifts mass from M_j to regional destinations R_k. Consequently, the outflow w_{ij}(t) toward global hubs declines monotonically, consistent with equation (6).

Proof sketch. Consider the value difference \Delta V = V_{M_j} - V_{R_k}. By the HJB equation, \Delta V depends negatively on the cost gap c_{ij}(t) - c_{ik}(t). As \alpha_j rises, c_{ij} increases, making \Delta V negative for a larger set of human‑capital states. A standard comparison argument then shows that the optimal policy switches from M_j to R_k for the marginal agents, redirecting flow. \square

