7. Macro Cooperation: Evolutionary Game of Regional Innovation

With global integration waning, countries in a geographic region must decide whether to invest in a shared innovation ecosystem.

7.1 Tripartite Evolutionary Game

We model three interacting populations—Government (G), University (U), Firm (F)—each choosing either Cooperate (C) or Defect (D). Their payoffs depend on the global demand for migrant labour D(t) derived in Theorem 1. When D is high, the outside option of exporting talent is attractive and defection yields a higher payoff. When D is low, regional synergies dominate.

Concretely, for player i \in \{G,U,F\},

\pi_i^C = b_i + \beta_i P_R - c_i, \qquad \pi_i^D = b_i + \gamma_i D(t) - f_i,
\tag{15}

where P_R is total regional productivity and the other parameters capture benefits and costs. This structure is inspired by the Triple Helix framework for government–university–industry collaboration [Etzkowitz & Leydesdorff, 2000], but departs from the extant literature by endogenising the attractiveness of the global market as a driver of defection.

7.2 Replicator Dynamics and ESS

Let x_i\in[0,1] be the fraction of cooperators in population i. The replicator dynamics are

\dot{x}_i = x_i(1-x_i)\bigl( \pi_i^C(\mathbf{x},D) - \bar{\pi}_i(\mathbf{x},D) \bigr).
\tag{16}

Theorem 4 (Evolutionary robust cooperation).
There exists a threshold D_c>0 such that for all D(t)<D_c, the all‑cooperate state (1,1,1) is an evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS). Moreover, as D(t) declines below D_c, the basin of attraction of (1,1,1) expands to cover the entire strategy space.

Proof. At (1,1,1), the payoff advantage of C over D is \pi_i^C - \pi_i^D = (\beta_i P_R - c_i) - (\gamma_i D - f_i). Define

D_c = \min_{i\in\{G,U,F\}} \frac{\beta_i P_R - c_i + f_i}{\gamma_i}.

For D<D_c, \pi_i^C > \pi_i^D for all i; C strictly dominates D. The Jacobian of (16) at (1,1,1) is diagonal with entries -\bigl(\pi_i^C - \pi_i^D\bigr) < 0, so the equilibrium is hyperbolic and asymptotically stable, implying ESS. Because Theorem 1 guarantees D(t)\to0 as A(t)\to K, the condition is eventually met and the system globally converges to full cooperation. \square

This theorem formalises the AI Regionalisation Principle: as AI erodes the global demand for migrant talent, the strategic environment flips from a defection trap to a cooperation optimum, making regional integration the rational evolutionary outcome.

