8. Optimal Transport and the Dynamic Transition Path

To trace the continuous reshaping of the migration matrix, we embed the flows in an entropy‑regularised optimal transport (OT) framework.

8.1 Static Equilibrium as Entropic OT

Given sending capacities S_i and receiving demands M_j(\alpha_j), the flow matrix solves

\min_{W\ge 0} \sum_{i,j} w_{ij} c_{ij} + \varepsilon \sum_{i,j} w_{ij}\log w_{ij},
\tag{17}

subject to \sum_j w_{ij}=S_i,\; \sum_i w_{ij}=M_j(\alpha_j). The migration cost is modelled as

c_{ij}(\alpha_j) = c_{ij}^0 + \lambda\,\alpha_j\, d_{ij},
\tag{18}

where d_{ij}=1 if i and j are on different continents and 0 otherwise, capturing the asymmetric rise in costs for distant, automated destinations.

Lemma 8.1 (Stability of Sinkhorn solution).
The unique solution W^*(\alpha) of the regularised OT problem is continuous in the cost matrix and hence in \bar{\alpha}, in the Wasserstein‑2 sense [Peyré & Cuturi, 2019]. Therefore, as automation rises, the flow matrix deforms smoothly, redistributing mass from intercontinental to regional routes.

8.2 Dynamic Schrödinger Bridge

The most likely evolution of the network between an initial state at t=0 and a predicted final regionalised state at t=T solves the Schrödinger bridge problem. This path minimises the entropic cost over the space of probability measures on paths and yields a continuous trajectory W(t) that necessarily passes through the percolation threshold \alpha_c characterised in Theorem 3. This construction provides a concrete realisation of the phase transition within the OT geometry.

