9. Network Control and Regional Resilience

After fragmentation, regional clusters aim to maximise their internal connectivity. We formulate this as a network design problem.

9.1 Maximising Algebraic Connectivity

Given a regional network R=(V_R,E_R) with Laplacian L_R, we add up to k new edges (e.g. joint AI labs, research centres) under a budget constraint to maximise \lambda_2(L_R). This is the algebraic connectivity maximisation problem:

\max_{E_{\text{new}}\subseteq V_R\times V_R,\, |E_{\text{new}}|\le k} \lambda_2\!\left(L_R + \sum_{(u,v)\in E_{\text{new}}} \mathbf{L}_{uv}\right),
\tag{19}

where \mathbf{L}_{uv} is the Laplacian of a single edge of unit weight. Although NP‑hard, this can be efficiently approximated by greedy addition guided by the Fiedler vector, directly identifying the bilateral investments that most enhance the region’s cohesion [Ghosh & Boyd, 2006].

9.2 Driver Nodes for Retention

Using structural controllability, we can identify the smallest set of “driver nodes” (countries or institutions) that must be directly influenced to steer the regional human‑capital distribution to a desired state. This offers a principled allocation algorithm for regional development funds.

