Appendix A. Task‑Based Production and Migrant Labour Demand

A.1. Cost‑Minimising Allocation

Fix a receiving country j with AI capability A. Factor prices are w_L (domestic skilled), w_M (migrant), and r (AI capital rental). Task x can be produced by domestic labour, migrants, or AI with productivities \gamma_L(x),\gamma_M(x),\gamma_A(x)>0 continuous on [0,1]. The unit costs are

c_L(x)=\frac{w_L}{\gamma_L(x)},\quad
c_M(x)=\frac{w_M}{\gamma_M(x)},\quad
c_K(x)=\frac{r}{\gamma_A(x)A}.

The firm assigns each task to the factor with the smallest unit cost, yielding a partition

\mathcal{T}_L=\{x: c_L(x)\le \min\{c_M(x),c_K(x)\}\},\quad
\mathcal{T}_M=\{x: c_M(x)\le \min\{c_L(x),c_K(x)\}\},\quad
\mathcal{T}_{AI}=\{x: c_K(x)\le \min\{c_L(x),c_M(x)\}\}.

A.2. Ordering by Comparative Advantage and Lemma 2.1

Assumption A.1 (Comparative advantage ordering).
The functions

z_{MA}(x)=\frac{\gamma_M(x)}{\gamma_A(x)},\qquad z_{ML}(x)=\frac{\gamma_M(x)}{\gamma_L(x)}

are strictly decreasing and continuous on [0,1].

Proof of Lemma 2.1.
The condition for a task to be performed by migrants is

\frac{w_M}{\gamma_M(x)}\le \frac{r}{\gamma_A(x)A}\quad\text{and}\quad
\frac{w_M}{\gamma_M(x)}\le \frac{w_L}{\gamma_L(x)}.

These are equivalent to

A\ge \frac{r}{w_M}z_{MA}(x),\qquad \frac{w_L}{w_M}\ge z_{ML}(x)^{-1}.

Because z_{MA} is strictly decreasing, the inequality A\ge (r/w_M)z_{MA}(x) defines an upper interval [0,x_{MA}] where

x_{MA}= \inf\{x: z_{MA}(x)< \frac{w_M}{r}A\}\quad(\text{with convention }x_{MA}=1\text{ if no such }x).

Similarly, because z_{ML} is decreasing, the condition w_L/w_M \ge 1/z_{ML}(x) is equivalent to z_{ML}(x)\ge w_M/w_L, which defines a lower interval [x_{ML},1] with

x_{ML}= \sup\{x: z_{ML}(x) \ge w_M/w_L\}.

Thus \mathcal{T}_M = [x_{ML}, x_{MA}]\cap[0,1], which is an interval (possibly empty). As A increases, x_{MA} decreases; x_{ML} is independent of A. Therefore the measure \mu(\mathcal{T}_M) = x_{MA}-x_{ML} is non‑increasing in A, strictly decreasing whenever the interior is non‑empty. \square

A.3. Derivation of Derived Demand and Theorem 1

The firm’s CES final output (1) with elasticity \sigma>1 implies that the demand for each task is iso‑elastic. Cost minimisation yields the price index

P = \Bigl( \int_0^1 p(x)^{1-\sigma}dx \Bigr)^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma}},

where p(x)=\min\{c_L(x),c_M(x),c_K(x)\}. The demand for migrant labour, in efficiency units, is the measure of tasks assigned to migrants multiplied by the output per task (normalised to unity). Hence

M_j(A,w_M) = \mu\bigl(\mathcal{T}_M(A,w_M)\bigr).
\tag{A.1}

The wage w_M equilibrates supply and demand. Let the supply of migrant labour to country j be given by an upward‑sloping function S(w_M), with S'>0. (A perfectly inelastic local supply is the limit S\to\infty slope.) The equilibrium condition is M_j(A,w_M)=S(w_M). By the implicit function theorem,

\frac{d w_M}{dA} = - \frac{ \partial M_j/\partial A }{ \partial M_j/\partial w_M - S'(w_M) }.

Because tasks are gross substitutes (\sigma>1), a rise in w_M increases the cost c_M relative to other factors, contracting \mathcal{T}_M; thus \partial M_j/\partial w_M<0. The denominator is negative, so the sign of dw_M/dA is the sign of \partial M_j/\partial A. From Lemma 2.1, \partial M_j/\partial A < 0 whenever \mathcal{T}_M has positive measure. Consequently, dw_M/dA < 0.

To prove Theorem 1, let A_c be the smallest capability such that \mathcal{T}_M has started shrinking. Then for all A>A_c, M_j is strictly decreasing and so is the equilibrium wage D_j\equiv w_M. Because the logistic trajectory eventually exceeds A_c, there exists t_c with A(t)>A_c for t>t_c, yielding dD_j/dt<0. \square

A.4. Elasticity of Migrant Demand and Network Flows

Define the baseline AI level A^0 and the proportional increase \alpha_j(t)=(A_j(t)-A^0)/A^0. Linearising M_j(A) around A^0 gives

M_j(A) \approx M_j^0\Bigl(1 - \eta_j \alpha_j\Bigr),

with elasticity \eta_j = -\frac{\partial \log M_j}{\partial \log A}\big|_{A^0}>0. Let s_{ij} be the fixed share of country i’s emigrants choosing destination j absent cost differences. Then the migration flow from i to j is

w_{ij}(t) = s_{ij}\, M_j(A_j(t)) \approx w_{ij}^0\bigl(1-\eta_j\alpha_j(t)\bigr),
\tag{A.2}

with w_{ij}^0 = s_{ij} M_j^0. Equation (A.2) is the first‑order approximation used throughout the network analysis.

