Appendix B. Spectral Dynamics and Proof of Theorem 2

B.1. Symmetrised Laplacian and Algebraic Connectivity

The weighted adjacency matrix W(t) is not symmetric in general. We analyse the undirected connectivity obtained by symmetrisation:

\widetilde{W}(t)=\frac{W(t)+W(t)^\top}{2}.

The weighted degree of node i is d_i(t)=\sum_j \widetilde{W}_{ij}(t). The unnormalised Laplacian is

L(t) = \operatorname{diag}(d_1,\dots,d_N) - \widetilde{W}(t).

Its eigenvalues are 0=\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\le\lambda_N. The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2 is strictly positive iff the graph is connected.

B.2. Cheeger Inequality for the Unnormalised Laplacian

For an undirected weighted graph, the Cheeger constant is

h_G = \min_{\substack{S\subset V\\ \operatorname{vol}(S)\le \operatorname{vol}(V)/2}} \frac{\operatorname{cut}(S,\bar{S})}{\operatorname{vol}(S)},

where \operatorname{cut}(S,\bar{S})=\sum_{i\in S,j\notin S} \widetilde{W}_{ij} and \operatorname{vol}(S)=\sum_{i\in S} d_i. The following inequality holds for the unnormalised Laplacian [Chung, 1997, Theorem 2.3]:

\frac{h_G^2}{2\, d_{\max}} \le \lambda_2 \le 2 h_G,
\tag{B.1}

with d_{\max}=\max_i d_i.

B.3. Monotonicity and Asymptotic Decay

Each automation level \alpha_j(t) is non‑decreasing by assumption, so every entry of \widetilde{W}(t) is non‑increasing. A classical result (e.g., Courant–Fischer min‑max) implies that adding non‑negative edge weights cannot decrease \lambda_2. Consequently, reducing weights monotonically cannot increase \lambda_2; thus \lambda_2(t) is non‑increasing for all t.

When \sup_j\alpha_j(t)\to1, all edges incident to high‑automation countries vanish. The graph eventually disconnects into isolated components, making h_G\to0. From the right‑hand inequality in (B.1), \lambda_2\to0. Combining with monotonicity gives \lim_{t\to\infty}\lambda_2(t)=0. \square

