Appendix C. Percolation on Directed Networks and Proof of Theorem 3

C.1. Edge Occupation Model

We work with the binary occupation model. Edge (i\to j) is present with probability

p_{ij}(\alpha_j) = \mathbf{1}_{(1-\eta_j\alpha_j)w_{ij}^0 \ge \theta},

or, more generally, a smooth decreasing function p_{ij}(\alpha_j) with p_{ij}(0)=1 and p_{ij}(1)=0. For analytical tractability we use the linear form

p_{ij} = \max\{0,\, 1 - c_{ij}\alpha_j\},

with constants c_{ij}>0. The average automation is \bar{\alpha}=N^{-1}\sum_j\alpha_j.

C.2. Non‑Backtracking Matrix and Giant Strongly Connected Component

For a directed graph with adjacency probabilities p_{ij}, define the non‑backtracking matrix B of size 2|E_{\text{pot}}| indexed by directed edges:

B_{(i\to j),(k\to l)} = \delta_{j,k}\,(1-\delta_{i,l})\,p_{kl}.
\tag{C.1}

Each entry represents the connection from edge i\to j to a subsequent edge k\to l that starts at node j and is not the reverse edge. The operator B tracks edge‑to‑edge transitions without immediate backtracking.

A giant strongly connected component (GSCC) exists if the spectral radius \rho(B)>1 [Karrer & Newman, 2011; Timár et al., 2017]. The relative size S of the GSCC is O(1) when \rho(B)>1 and o(1) when \rho(B)<1, with a continuous phase transition at \rho(B)=1.

C.3. Threshold Derivation

As \bar{\alpha} increases, all p_{ij} decrease, so each entry of B is non‑increasing. The spectral radius \rho(B) is a continuous, monotone function of \bar{\alpha}. At \bar{\alpha}=0, \rho(B)=\rho(B_0)>1 by strong connectivity of the initial network. At \bar{\alpha}=1, all edges vanish, giving \rho(B)=0. By the intermediate value theorem, there exists a unique \alpha_c\in(0,1) such that \rho(B(\alpha_c))=1. This \alpha_c is the critical percolation threshold.

For a network where p_{ij} depends only on \bar{\alpha} and is approximately homogeneous, we can write p_{ij}=p(\bar{\alpha}). In the large‑N limit, the non‑backtracking matrix of a complete directed graph with edge probability p has largest eigenvalue \rho(B)= p\,\rho(B_0), where B_0 is the matrix when p=1. Setting p\rho(B_0)=1 yields p_c=1/\rho(B_0). With p=1-\bar{\alpha}, we obtain the mean‑field estimate

\alpha_c \approx 1 - \frac{1}{\rho(B_0)}.

If the initial network is scale‑free with degree exponent \gamma<3, it is known that \rho(B_0)\to\infty as N\to\infty, implying \alpha_c\to1; such networks are ultra‑resilient. Conversely, for \gamma>3 the threshold remains bounded away from 1. This structural dependence yields the testable prediction that highly heterogeneous migration networks persist longer under automation. \square

