Appendix D. Mean‑Field Game of Human‑Capital Migration

D.1. Agent Problem and PDE System

Consider a representative agent in country i with human capital h_t. The control set is \mathcal{U}=\{S, M_j, R_k\}. Migration costs are c_{ij}(t)=c_0/(1-\alpha_j(t)) for destination j and c_{ik}=c_0 (constant) for regional partner k. The wage functions are w_S(h,\mu), w_{M_j}(h,\mu), w_{R_k}(h,\mu), assumed smooth and strictly increasing in h.

The agent solves

\sup_{u} \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ \int_0^\infty e^{-\rho t}\bigl( w_{u}(h_t,\mu_t) - c_{iu}(t) \bigr) dt \Bigr],

subject to dh_t = (\mu_h(u)-\delta h_t)dt + \sigma dW_t. The value function V(h,t) satisfies the HJB equation

-\partial_t V + \rho V = \max_{u}\Bigl\{ w_{u}(h,\mu) - c_{iu}(t) + (\mu_h(u)-\delta h)\partial_h V + \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\partial_{hh} V \Bigr\}.
\tag{D.1}

Given the optimal feedback control u^*(h,t), the density m(h,t) of agents with human capital h evolves by the Fokker–Planck equation

\partial_t m + \partial_h\bigl[ (\mu_h(u^*)-\delta h)m \bigr] - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}\partial_{hh} m = 0,
\tag{D.2}

and the mean field is \mu_t = m(\cdot,t).

D.2. Shift in Optimal Control (Proposition 1)

Assumption D.1 (Monotonicity and separability).
The wage functions satisfy w_{M_j}(h,\mu)-w_{R_k}(h,\mu) is independent of \mu and strictly decreasing in h. The migration cost difference \Delta c_{j}(t)=c_{ij}(t)-c_{ik} is non‑decreasing in t (as automation rises).

Consider the differential value \Delta V(h,t) = V_{M_j}(h,t) - V_{R_k}(h,t), where V_u solves (D.1) with the control fixed to u. Using the HJB equation and the envelope condition, one can show that \Delta V satisfies a linear parabolic inequality. By a standard comparison principle, if \Delta c_j(t) increases, the region of h where \Delta V>0 (i.e., where migration to j is preferred) shrinks. Consequently, the proportion of agents choosing M_j falls, while the proportion choosing R_k rises. This yields the monotonic shift in migration flows asserted in Proposition 1. A rigorous proof can be constructed using viscosity solutions and barrier arguments; see e.g. [Lasry & Lions, 2007; Guéant et al., 2011] for similar threshold effects in MFG. \square

