Appendix E. Evolutionary Game of Regional Cooperation (Theorem 4)

E.1. Replicator Dynamics Setup

Three populations (G, U, F) with fractions x_G,x_U,x_F of cooperators. Payoffs for cooperation and defection are given by (15). The average payoff in population i is \bar{\pi}_i = x_i\pi_i^C + (1-x_i)\pi_i^D. The replicator dynamics read

\dot{x}_i = x_i(1-x_i)\bigl( \pi_i^C - \pi_i^D \bigr) = x_i(1-x_i)\bigl( \beta_i P_R - c_i + f_i - \gamma_i D \bigr),
\tag{E.1}

where P_R is taken as a fixed parameter (regional productivity is a public good independent of the strategic choices within this game; alternatively one could endogenise it without changing the qualitative result as long as cooperation increases it, which would only strengthen the conclusion).

E.2. Equilibrium Analysis

The rest points are the vertices of the unit cube. The all‑cooperate state (1,1,1) is an equilibrium. The Jacobian of (E.1) at (1,1,1) is diagonal with entries

J_{ii} = -\bigl( \beta_i P_R - c_i + f_i - \gamma_i D \bigr),

all other entries zero. Hence the eigenvalues are -\Delta_i where \Delta_i = \pi_i^C - \pi_i^D. If \Delta_i>0 for all i, all eigenvalues are negative and the equilibrium is hyperbolic and asymptotically stable. Moreover, since the payoff differences are constant and independent of x, the replicator dynamics are decoupled logistic equations. The basin of attraction of (1,1,1) is the whole interior (0,1]^3 whenever \Delta_i>0. The condition \Delta_i>0 is equivalent to D < (\beta_i P_R - c_i + f_i)/\gamma_i. Define

D_c = \min_{i\in\{G,U,F\}} \frac{\beta_i P_R - c_i + f_i}{\gamma_i}.

For D<D_c, all \Delta_i>0, hence (1,1,1) is globally asymptotically stable. By the definition of an evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS) in multi‑population games, strict Nash equilibrium suffices: with \pi_i^C > \pi_i^D for each population, any alternative strategy achieves a lower payoff against the incumbent population state (1,1,1). Thus (1,1,1) is an ESS. \square

