Appendix F. Entropic Optimal Transport and the Transition Path

F.1. Static Problem

Given sending capacities S_i and receiving demands M_j(\alpha_j), the regularised Kantorovich problem is

\min_{W\ge 0} \sum_{i,j} w_{ij} c_{ij}(\alpha_j) + \varepsilon \sum_{i,j} w_{ij}\log w_{ij}
\tag{F.1}

subject to \sum_j w_{ij}=S_i, \sum_i w_{ij}=M_j(\alpha_j). The unique solution can be computed by the Sinkhorn algorithm. The cost matrix is specified as

c_{ij}(\alpha_j) = c_{ij}^0 + \lambda \alpha_j d_{ij},

with d_{ij}=1 for intercontinental pairs and 0 for regional pairs, \lambda>0.

F.2. Stability Lemma

Lemma F.1 (Continuity in \alpha).
The solution W^*(\alpha) of (F.1) is continuous in the cost matrix and hence in \bar{\alpha}, with respect to the Wasserstein‑2 metric on the space of probability matrices. This follows from the strict convexity of the entropy term and the Berge maximum theorem [Peyré & Cuturi, 2019, Prop. 4.2].

Thus, as \alpha varies continuously, the flow matrix deforms smoothly, shifting mass from distant destinations (where costs rise) to regional partners. The trajectory W(t) must therefore pass through the percolation threshold \alpha_c, realising the topological phase transition.

F.3. Schrödinger Bridge

The dynamic evolution linking an initial flow matrix W(0) to a predicted regionalised final matrix W(T) that minimises the entropic cost over path space is the solution of the Schrödinger bridge problem. The existence of a continuous path in the space of measures ensures that the network transitions smoothly through the fragmentation threshold. \square

