Appendix H. Information‑Theoretic Interpretation

Define the random variables \mathbf{S} (skill set of a migrant) and \mathbf{T} (task requirement in the destination). The mutual information I(\mathbf{S};\mathbf{T}) measures how much knowing \mathbf{S} reduces uncertainty about \mathbf{T}. Before widespread AI, many tasks could only be performed by humans with specific tacit knowledge; hence I was high.

When AI capital is introduced, tasks can be performed by either humans or AI. The conditional mutual information given AI availability is

I(\mathbf{S};\mathbf{T}\mid \text{AI}) = H(\mathbf{T}\mid \text{AI}) - H(\mathbf{T}\mid \mathbf{S},\text{AI}),

where H denotes entropy. As AI becomes capable of executing a wider range of cognitive tasks, the residual uncertainty about \mathbf{T} after observing \mathbf{S} (and knowing that AI is available) shrinks, because the AI itself can perform the task without the specific skill. Therefore I(\mathbf{S};\mathbf{T}\mid \text{AI}) decreases. The channel capacity of the migration network, interpreted as the maximum mutual information over input distributions, falls. This decline manifests as the vanishing of edge weights w_{ij}, providing an information‑theoretic rationale for the network disintegration. \square

